$\dfrac{\sqrt{x}}{e^x}$ is the quotient of two, more basic, expressions: $\sqrt{x}$ and $e^x$.

Therefore, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ can be found using **the quotient rule**:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}\right]&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)]v(x)-u(x)\dfrac{d}{dx}[v(x)]}{[v(x)]^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}
\end{align}$

Let's differentiate!

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{dy}{dx}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{x}}{e^x}\right)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}(\sqrt{x})e^x-\sqrt{x}\dfrac{d}{dx}(e^x)}{(e^x)^2}&&\gray{\text{The quotient rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac1{2\sqrt{x}}\cdot e^x-\sqrt{x}\cdot e^x}{(e^x)^2}&&\gray{\text{Differentiate }\sqrt{x}\text{ and }e^x}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{e^x}\left(\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}-\sqrt{x}\right)}{(e^x)^\cancel{2}}&&\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{1-2x}{2\sqrt{x}}}{e^x}&&\gray{\text{Combine numerator}}
\\\\
&={\dfrac{1-2x}{2e^x\sqrt{x}}}&&\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\end{align}$
